{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"terrible-compulsory-cod","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context\n\n`fixture_selection._fire_rows` (landed via ticket 549b / Gerrit 2552) groups a criterion's\nmatched findings by `norm_id` with a direct subscript:\n\n```python\nby_norm.setdefault(str(finding[\"norm_id\"]), []).append((review, finding))\n```\n\nOn the REAL committed plan-review corpus this raises `KeyError: 'norm_id'`. Plan-review's\nsidecar `_slim` is a field-SELECT (confirmed in bug 884d-ece3-c961-4959): it emits `norm_id`\nonly for normalized (LLM) findings and OMITS it for deterministic (`tier: DET`) findings. Over\nthe tickets tracker, 1437 of 79912 criterion-naming findings carry no `norm_id` key. The first\nsuch finding aborts the entire candidate-selection pass, so the fixture-mining pipeline\n(epic 65dd-e93b-666a-4caa) cannot run over real history at all — it blocks the ticket 67aa\nlive admission run (AC10).\n\nSibling access in the same module is already defensive (`_findings_for_criterion` uses\n`f.get(\"criteria\")`); only the fire-grouping subscript is not. A finding with no `norm_id` has\nno normalized cross-review identity, so it cannot participate in `by_norm` reproduction-consensus\ngrouping (nor in the `norm_id`-keyed `author_response` set); it must be excluded from the\nnorm-grouped fire path rather than crash it.\n\n## Approach\n\nIn `_fire_rows`, read `norm_id` defensively and skip findings that lack it (they cannot be\nnorm-grouped). This is a minimal, behavior-preserving change: for every finding that HAS a\n`norm_id`, grouping/signals/candidate emission are byte-identical to today; only the previously\ncrashing norm-less findings are now excluded. No other function changes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `_fire_rows` no longer raises on a finding that names the criterion but has no `norm_id`\n      key: a `select_candidates` run whose eligible reviews include a criterion-naming finding\n      with no `norm_id` (alongside norm-bearing ones) returns rows without error (`pytest`).\n- [ ] A finding lacking `norm_id` contributes NO fire candidate and does not perturb the\n      candidates produced by norm-bearing findings for the same criterion: the emitted fire\n      rows for a mixed eligible set equal the rows produced when the norm-less finding is\n      absent (`pytest`).\n- [ ] Existing norm-bearing selection behavior is unchanged: the module's current\n      `test_fixture_selection` suite still passes (`pytest`).\n- [ ] Selection remains deterministic — two `select_candidates` runs over the same eligible\n      set return byte-identical manifest rows (`pytest`).\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n`make lint` / `make typecheck` / targeted `pytest` pass; the fix lands through Gerrit\n(`LLM-Review +1` and `Verified +1`); the fixture-mining selection pass runs over the real\ncorpus without the `norm_id` KeyError.\n\n## File impact\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/evals/fixture_selection.py` — defensive `norm_id` read + skip in `_fire_rows`.\n- `tests/unit/test_fixture_selection.py` — held-out regression for the norm-less finding.","id":"57c4-4834-2a7a-4a05","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"fixture_selection._fire_rows KeyError on findings without norm_id blocks real-corpus selection"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788443599471530001,"uuid":"bf215ea1-6598-42d9-8d91-397118ae3143"}